WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.093
Module one:

2
00:00:01.093 --> 00:00:02.512
blockchain and coins.

3
00:00:02.512 --> 00:00:05.958
We will see how a shared record of transfers works,

4
00:00:05.958 --> 00:00:11.566
who decides which block comes next, how bitcoin is issued, and how smart contracts,

5
00:00:11.566 --> 00:00:14.606
tokens, stablecoins and second layers differ.

6
00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:18.242
A bank keeps its record of money to itself.

7
00:00:18.242 --> 00:00:22.963
A blockchain does the same, but the record is kept by thousands of computers,

8
00:00:22.963 --> 00:00:24.802
and none of them is in charge.

9
00:00:24.802 --> 00:00:28.971
Transfers are gathered into blocks, and blocks are linked by hashes.

10
00:00:28.971 --> 00:00:30.933
A hash is a fingerprint of data:

11
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:34.182
change one letter, and the whole fingerprint changes.

12
00:00:35.182 --> 00:00:39.898
In the lesson you can change one digit in a block and see how the hash changes.

13
00:00:39.898 --> 00:00:42.705
Each block stores the hash of the previous one.

14
00:00:42.705 --> 00:00:46.645
So forging an old record means recalculating every block after it,

15
00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:49.630
faster than the rest of the network adds new ones.

16
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.536
Who adds the next block is decided by consensus.

17
00:00:53.536 --> 00:00:58.621
In proof of work, the right goes to whoever first finds the required number by brute

18
00:00:58.621 --> 00:01:01.467
force, and that costs hardware and electricity.

19
00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:05.825
In proof of stake, blocks are proposed by validators who put up a stake,

20
00:01:05.825 --> 00:01:08.126
and for cheating they lose part of it.

21
00:01:09.126 --> 00:01:11.196
Both methods share one weak spot:

22
00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:12.450
the majority attack.

23
00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.155
Whoever controls more than half of the power, or a large part of the stake,

24
00:01:17.155 --> 00:01:20.730
can rewrite recent blocks and spend the same coins twice.

25
00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:25.623
In big networks this costs billions; in small ones such attacks have happened.

26
00:01:25.623 --> 00:01:30.390
That is why an exchange credits a transfer only after several confirmations.

27
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.308
New bitcoins appear only as a block reward.

28
00:01:34.308 --> 00:01:38.990
Every 210,000 blocks, roughly every four years, the reward is halved.

29
00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.451
There will be just under 21 million coins in total,

30
00:01:42.451 --> 00:01:45.437
and more than 95 percent are already issued.

31
00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:48.762
But limited issuance does not force the price up:

32
00:01:48.762 --> 00:01:52.630
it is known in advance, and the price is moved by demand.

33
00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.001
Ethereum stores not only transfers but also programs:

34
00:01:57.001 --> 00:01:58.019
smart contracts.

35
00:01:58.019 --> 00:02:00.372
A contract is like a vending machine:

36
00:02:00.372 --> 00:02:04.189
the rules are written in code and are the same for everyone.

37
00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:06.797
It cannot be secretly changed or stopped.

38
00:02:06.797 --> 00:02:10.168
But a bug in the code cannot be quickly fixed either:

39
00:02:10.168 --> 00:02:13.094
the network will execute it just as precisely.

40
00:02:14.094 --> 00:02:16.848
Anyone can issue a token in a few minutes,

41
00:02:16.848 --> 00:02:19.864
so a token by itself says nothing about value.

42
00:02:19.864 --> 00:02:22.945
Stablecoins try to be worth exactly one dollar.

43
00:02:22.945 --> 00:02:25.961
Money-backed ones rely on the issuing company,

44
00:02:25.961 --> 00:02:29.043
crypto-backed ones on collateral in a contract,

45
00:02:29.043 --> 00:02:32.846
algorithmic ones on a promise to swap for their own token.

46
00:02:32.846 --> 00:02:35.337
In May 2022 that is how UST collapsed,

47
00:02:35.337 --> 00:02:38.550
and tens of billions of dollars vanished with it.

48
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.677
The network's work is not free.

49
00:02:41.677 --> 00:02:44.009
In Ethereum it is measured in gas:

50
00:02:44.009 --> 00:02:48.194
a simple transfer is 21,000 units, a swap several times more.

51
00:02:48.194 --> 00:02:51.145
The fee is gas multiplied by the gas price.

52
00:02:51.145 --> 00:02:55.261
The base part is burned, the rest is a tip to the validator.

53
00:02:55.261 --> 00:02:59.446
When everyone wants to get into a block, the gas price rises.

54
00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:02.578
Hence the idea of a second layer.

55
00:03:02.578 --> 00:03:07.940
A rollup gathers many transfers into a batch and writes the whole batch to the main

56
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.235
network at once, and the fee is shared by everyone.

57
00:03:11.235 --> 00:03:15.951
Optimistic rollups treat a batch as valid until someone proves otherwise,

58
00:03:15.951 --> 00:03:18.019
so a withdrawal can take a week.

59
00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:20.538
ZK rollups attach a mathematical proof.

60
00:03:20.538 --> 00:03:23.381
The price of cheapness is new links of risk:

61
00:03:23.381 --> 00:03:25.190
the operator and the bridge.

62
00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.214
The main points.

63
00:03:27.214 --> 00:03:31.376
A blockchain is a shared record that cannot be quietly rewritten.

64
00:03:31.376 --> 00:03:34.257
Who adds a block is decided by work or stake.

65
00:03:34.257 --> 00:03:39.251
And network coins, tokens, stablecoins and second layers are different things,

66
00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:43.029
each with its own answer to the question of whom you trust.

67
00:03:43.029 --> 00:03:45.846
Details are in the lessons below this video.
